# Riverside Malls Smart Trolley Pilot — Product One-Pager

## Recommendation

Riverside Malls should launch a paid pilot of 25 smart trolleys at **£200 per trolley per month**.

At this price, the pilot is expected to generate a monthly profit of **£1,000** and break even at approximately **19 trolleys**. This gives Riverside Malls some financial margin while keeping the starting price below the £250 and £300 alternatives.

## Target Users

The main users are:

- Shopping-centre operators
- Supermarkets and large retailers
- Customers shopping inside the mall
- Store staff responsible for customer support and trolley management

The paying customer in the base business model is the operator using the trolley system.

## Customer Problem

Large shopping centres want to improve the customer experience, but shoppers can find it difficult to locate products, manage their baskets and move efficiently around stores.

Retailers also have limited information about how smart trolleys are being used, whether batteries are low and where support may be needed.

## Core Job to Be Done

The smart trolley should help customers complete their shopping more easily while giving operators a reliable way to manage and monitor the trolley fleet.

The core job is:

> Help shoppers find products and manage their shopping journey while giving operators useful trolley data.

## Minimum Viable Product

The first version of the product should include only the features needed to test whether customers and operators find the service useful.

The MVP will include:

- Product search
- Product and aisle information
- A digital shopping basket
- Live basket totals
- Basic shopping suggestions
- Trolley position tracking
- Battery-level monitoring
- Basket-weight telemetry
- Low-battery alerts
- A simple operator dashboard
- Basic technical support during the pilot

## Features Deliberately Left Out

The pilot will not initially include:

- Payment processing through the trolley
- Facial recognition
- Personalised advertising
- Advanced customer accounts
- Automated checkout
- Full indoor route optimisation
- Loyalty-scheme integration
- Detailed customer behaviour tracking
- Multi-store rollout
- Advanced artificial intelligence recommendations

These features are left out because they would increase development cost, complexity and security risk before the basic value of the trolley system has been proven.

## Revenue Model

### Base Revenue

The main revenue counted in the financial model is the monthly operator fee.

For 25 trolleys at £200 each:

```text
25 × £200 = £5,000 monthly revenue
```

With variable costs of £40 per trolley and fixed monthly costs of £3,000:

```text
Variable costs = 25 × £40 = £1,000
Total costs = £1,000 + £3,000 = £4,000
Monthly profit = £5,000 − £4,000 = £1,000
```

This operator fee is the only revenue included in the base case because it is the most reliable and directly linked to the pilot.

### Possible Upside Revenue

Additional revenue could come from:

- Shopper hire fees
- Sponsored product recommendations
- Retail advertising
- Premium analytics for operators
- Maintenance packages
- Integration fees
- Future licensing to other shopping centres

These are treated as possible upside and are not included in the main profit calculation because they have not yet been proven.

## Pilot Success Measures

The pilot should be judged using:

- Number of active trolleys
- Percentage of available trolleys used
- Customer satisfaction
- Number of completed shopping sessions
- Frequency of technical problems
- Support cost per trolley
- Battery reliability
- Operator willingness to continue paying
- Customer willingness to use the trolleys again

## Main Risks

### Low adoption

Customers may prefer ordinary trolleys or may find the technology difficult to use.

**Mitigation:** provide clear instructions, staff support and a short onboarding process.

### Price resistance

Operators may consider £200 per trolley too expensive.

**Mitigation:** run the pilot at £200 and collect evidence about savings, customer satisfaction and usage before discussing higher prices.

### Higher support costs

Technical problems may make the real variable cost higher than the assumed £40.

**Mitigation:** track maintenance and support costs throughout the pilot and run a sensitivity analysis.

### Reliability problems

Poor battery life, inaccurate location data or software failures could reduce trust in the system.

**Mitigation:** monitor faults, maintain backup trolleys and test the system before full rollout.

### Security and privacy concerns

Location data and customer activity could create privacy or security risks.

**Mitigation:** collect only necessary data, avoid storing personal information and secure the API and operator dashboard.

## Pilot Scope

The pilot will run with 25 smart trolleys at Riverside Malls.

The purpose is to test:

- Whether shoppers use the trolleys
- Whether the system improves the shopping experience
- Whether the operator is willing to pay
- Whether support and maintenance costs remain manageable
- Whether the £200 monthly price produces a sustainable margin

## Final Product Positioning

The smart trolley should be positioned as a practical retail service rather than a technology demonstration.

Its value is that it helps shoppers complete their trips more easily while giving mall operators a manageable and measurable digital trolley system.